IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `gaq-common`

-- Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Queensland) -- chapter 4 as a claimant meets it once an administrator
-- manages the claim or its proceeds: conflict transactions (s 37, with s 152), payment of the administrator (ss 47, 48),
-- gifts (s 54), compensation or an account of profits from the administrator and its time limit (s 59, with s 58; a
-- regulative rule), a lost benefit in the estate (s 60), a beneficiary's interest in proceeds (ss 60A, 60C; a
-- regulative rule), and accounts and audit (s 153).
--
-- SEAT: the adult whose money (for example a compensation payout) an administrator manages; for s 59 after the adult's
-- death, the adult's estate; for ss 60, 60A and 60C, a person who takes from the adult's estate. "The court" in ss 60
-- and 60C is the Supreme Court (sch 4); in s 59 it is "any court" (s 59(10)).
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Chapter 4: what my administrator may do with my money, and my remedies`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 37, 152 -- conflict transactions`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 37(1): "An administrator for an adult may enter into a conflict transaction only if the tribunal has authorised the
-- transaction, conflict transactions of that type or conflict transactions generally." (2): "A conflict transaction is
-- a transaction in which there may be conflict, or which results in conflict, between-- (a) the duty of an administrator
-- towards the adult; and (b) either-- (i) the interests of the administrator or a person in a close personal or business
-- relationship with the administrator; or (ii) another duty of the administrator." (3): "However, a transaction is not a
-- conflict transaction merely because-- (a) the administrator is related to the adult; or (b) the administrator may be a
-- beneficiary of the adult's estate ...; or (c) [joint property dealings]". (4): a gift under s 54 is not one. Example
-- 6: acting under s 55 for dependants is not one. s 152(2), (3): the tribunal may authorise it retrospectively, and it
-- is then valid as if authorised before; (4): until then, the administrator "has acted contrary to section 37(1)".
-- The conflict test and the "merely because" carve-out are folded into one fact: "the only possible conflict is that
-- the administrator is my relative, a possible beneficiary, or a joint owner dealing with joint property".
DECLARE `Is the transaction allowed` IS ONE OF
    `it is not a conflict transaction: s 37 does not restrict it`
    `it is a conflict transaction the tribunal authorised beforehand: allowed (ss 37(1), 152(1))`
    `it is a conflict transaction the tribunal authorised afterwards: valid as if authorised beforehand (s 152(2), (3))`
    `it is a conflict transaction without the tribunal's authorisation: your administrator has acted contrary to s 37(1) (s 152(4)); you may seek compensation or an account of profits (s 59)`

GIVEN `the transaction may set my administrator's duty to me against their own or a close person's interests, or another duty` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the only such conflict is that they are my relative, a possible beneficiary, or a joint owner dealing with joint property` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is a gift under s 54 or provision for my dependants under s 55` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the tribunal authorised it beforehand` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the tribunal authorised it afterwards` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Is the transaction allowed`
`s 37 -- conflict transaction` `the transaction may set my administrator's duty to me against their own or a close person's interests, or another duty` `the only such conflict is that they are my relative, a possible beneficiary, or a joint owner dealing with joint property` `it is a gift under s 54 or provision for my dependants under s 55` `the tribunal authorised it beforehand` `the tribunal authorised it afterwards` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the transaction may set my administrator's duty to me against their own or a close person's interests, or another duty`)
       OR `the only such conflict is that they are my relative, a possible beneficiary, or a joint owner dealing with joint property`
       OR `it is a gift under s 54 or provision for my dependants under s 55`
    THEN `it is not a conflict transaction: s 37 does not restrict it`
    ELSE IF `the tribunal authorised it beforehand`
         THEN `it is a conflict transaction the tribunal authorised beforehand: allowed (ss 37(1), 152(1))`
         ELSE IF `the tribunal authorised it afterwards`
              THEN `it is a conflict transaction the tribunal authorised afterwards: valid as if authorised beforehand (s 152(2), (3))`
              ELSE `it is a conflict transaction without the tribunal's authorisation: your administrator has acted contrary to s 37(1) (s 152(4)); you may seek compensation or an account of profits (s 59)`

@export My administrator wants to deal with my money or property in a way that may benefit them (for example buying my car, lending my payout to a friend, paying their own expenses). Is that allowed (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) ss 37, 152)?
GIVEN `the transaction may set my administrator's duty to me against their own or a close person's interests, or another duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Could the transaction set your administrator's duty to you against their own interests, the interests of someone close to them personally or in business, or another duty they have?
      `the only such conflict is that they are my relative, a possible beneficiary, or a joint owner dealing with joint property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the only possible conflict that the administrator is related to you, may inherit from you, or is dealing with property you hold jointly with them?
      `it is a gift under s 54 or provision for my dependants under s 55` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a permitted gift or donation of your property, or provision for your dependants' needs?
      `the tribunal authorised it beforehand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the tribunal authorise this transaction, transactions of this type, or conflict transactions generally, before it was entered into?
      `the tribunal authorised it afterwards` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the tribunal authorised the transaction after it was entered into?
GIVETH A `Is the transaction allowed`
DECIDE `Is my administrator's transaction allowed question` IS
    `s 37 -- conflict transaction` `the transaction may set my administrator's duty to me against their own or a close person's interests, or another duty` `the only such conflict is that they are my relative, a possible beneficiary, or a joint owner dealing with joint property` `it is a gift under s 54 or provision for my dependants under s 55` `the tribunal authorised it beforehand` `the tribunal authorised it afterwards`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 47, 48 -- paying my administrator from my money`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 47: "A guardian or administrator for an adult is entitled to reimbursement from the adult of the reasonable expenses
-- incurred in acting as guardian or administrator." s 48(1): "An administrator for an adult is entitled to remuneration
-- from the adult if the tribunal makes an order that the administrator is to be remunerated by the adult." (2): only if
-- "the administrator carries on a business providing professional services". (3): not more than what the tribunal
-- considers fair and reasonable. (4): the public trustee's and trustee companies' remuneration under other Acts is not
-- affected (not encoded: other Acts).
DECLARE `What my administrator wants paid` IS ONE OF
    `reimbursement of expenses`
    `remuneration for their work`

DECLARE `Can my administrator be paid from my money` IS ONE OF
    `yes: reasonable expenses incurred in acting as your administrator are reimbursed from you (s 47)`
    `no: s 47 reimburses only reasonable expenses incurred in acting as your administrator`
    `yes: the tribunal has ordered remuneration, capped at what it considers fair and reasonable (s 48(1), (3))`
    `no: remuneration is payable only if the tribunal orders it (s 48(1))`
    `no: the tribunal may order remuneration only for an administrator who carries on a business providing professional services (s 48(2))`

GIVEN `what is claimed` IS A `What my administrator wants paid`
      `the expenses are reasonable and incurred in acting as my administrator` IS A BOOLEAN
      `my administrator carries on a business providing professional services` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the tribunal ordered remuneration` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can my administrator be paid from my money`
`ss 47, 48 -- payment of the administrator` `what is claimed` `the expenses are reasonable and incurred in acting as my administrator` `my administrator carries on a business providing professional services` `the tribunal ordered remuneration` MEANS
    CONSIDER `what is claimed`
    WHEN `reimbursement of expenses` THEN
        IF `the expenses are reasonable and incurred in acting as my administrator`
        THEN `yes: reasonable expenses incurred in acting as your administrator are reimbursed from you (s 47)`
        ELSE `no: s 47 reimburses only reasonable expenses incurred in acting as your administrator`
    WHEN `remuneration for their work` THEN
        IF NOT `my administrator carries on a business providing professional services`
        THEN `no: the tribunal may order remuneration only for an administrator who carries on a business providing professional services (s 48(2))`
        ELSE IF `the tribunal ordered remuneration`
             THEN `yes: the tribunal has ordered remuneration, capped at what it considers fair and reasonable (s 48(1), (3))`
             ELSE `no: remuneration is payable only if the tribunal orders it (s 48(1))`

@export Can my administrator be paid, or reimbursed, out of my money or compensation (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) ss 47, 48)?
GIVEN `what is claimed` IS A `What my administrator wants paid` @desc What your administrator wants: reimbursement of expenses, or remuneration for their work
      `the expenses are reasonable and incurred in acting as my administrator` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For expenses: are they reasonable, and incurred in acting as your administrator?
      `my administrator carries on a business providing professional services` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For remuneration: does your administrator carry on a business providing professional services?
      `the tribunal ordered remuneration` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For remuneration: has the tribunal ordered that your administrator be remunerated by you?
GIVETH A `Can my administrator be paid from my money`
DECIDE `Can my administrator be paid from my money question` IS
    `ss 47, 48 -- payment of the administrator` `what is claimed` `the expenses are reasonable and incurred in acting as my administrator` `my administrator carries on a business providing professional services` `the tribunal ordered remuneration`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 54 -- gifts and donations of my property`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 54(1): "Unless the tribunal orders otherwise, an administrator for an adult may give away or donate the adult's
-- property only if-- (a) the gift or donation is-- (i) a gift or donation of the nature the adult made when the adult had
-- capacity; or (ii) a gift or donation of the nature the adult might reasonably be expected to make; and (b) the value
-- of the gift or donation is not more than what is reasonable having regard to all the circumstances and, in particular,
-- the adult's financial circumstances." (2): the administrator or a connected charity may receive one. "only if": JUST
-- FALSE outside; a tribunal order displaces the test (the answer then follows the order, not encoded).
DECLARE `Can my administrator give away my property` IS ONE OF
    `the tribunal's order governs: s 54(1) applies unless the tribunal orders otherwise`
    `yes: a gift of a kind you made or might reasonably be expected to make, of a reasonable value; the administrator may even receive it (s 54(1), (2))`
    `no: the gift is not of a kind you made when you had capacity or might reasonably be expected to make (s 54(1)(a))`
    `no: the value is more than is reasonable, having regard in particular to your financial circumstances (s 54(1)(b))`

GIVEN `the tribunal has ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is a gift of a kind I made with capacity or might reasonably be expected to make` IS A BOOLEAN
      `its value is reasonable in my financial circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can my administrator give away my property`
`s 54 -- gifts and donations` `the tribunal has ordered otherwise` `it is a gift of a kind I made with capacity or might reasonably be expected to make` `its value is reasonable in my financial circumstances` MEANS
    IF `the tribunal has ordered otherwise`
    THEN `the tribunal's order governs: s 54(1) applies unless the tribunal orders otherwise`
    ELSE IF NOT `it is a gift of a kind I made with capacity or might reasonably be expected to make`
         THEN `no: the gift is not of a kind you made when you had capacity or might reasonably be expected to make (s 54(1)(a))`
         ELSE IF NOT `its value is reasonable in my financial circumstances`
              THEN `no: the value is more than is reasonable, having regard in particular to your financial circumstances (s 54(1)(b))`
              ELSE `yes: a gift of a kind you made or might reasonably be expected to make, of a reasonable value; the administrator may even receive it (s 54(1), (2))`

@export Can my administrator give away or donate my money or property (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 54)?
GIVEN `the tribunal has ordered otherwise` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the tribunal made an order about gifts by your administrator?
      `it is a gift of a kind I made with capacity or might reasonably be expected to make` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a gift or donation of a kind you made when you had capacity, or might reasonably be expected to make?
      `its value is reasonable in my financial circumstances` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is its value no more than is reasonable in all the circumstances, in particular your financial circumstances?
GIVETH A `Can my administrator give away my property`
DECIDE `Can my administrator give away my property question` IS
    `s 54 -- gifts and donations` `the tribunal has ordered otherwise` `it is a gift of a kind I made with capacity or might reasonably be expected to make` `its value is reasonable in my financial circumstances`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 59 (with s 58) -- compensation or an account of profits from my administrator`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 59(1): "The tribunal or a court may order a guardian or administrator for an adult (an appointee) to pay an amount
-- to the adult or, if the adult has died, the adult's estate-- (a) to compensate for a loss caused by the appointee's
-- failure to comply with this Act in the exercise of a power; or (b) to account for any profits the appointee has
-- accrued as a result of the appointee's failure to comply with this Act in the exercise of a power." (2): not both for
-- the same exercise of power. (3): even if convicted. (4): "even if the appointee's appointment has ended". (9): an
-- amount paid "must be taken into account in assessing damages in a later civil proceeding". s 58: a court may relieve
-- an appointee who "acted honestly and reasonably and ought fairly to be excused" of all or part of personal liability
-- for a contravention. "may order": the tribunal's or court's power, answered conditionally.
DECLARE `Can I get compensation from my administrator` IS ONE OF
    `no: s 59 needs a failure by the administrator to comply with the Act in exercising a power`
    `no: the failure caused you no loss and brought the administrator no profit, so there is nothing for s 59 to order`
    `yes: the tribunal or a court may order the administrator to compensate your loss, or to account for their profits, but not both for the same exercise of power (s 59(1), (2)); this holds even if the appointment has ended or they were convicted (s 59(3), (4)); a court may relieve an administrator who acted honestly and reasonably (s 58); an amount paid counts against damages in a later civil claim (s 59(9))`

GIVEN `my administrator failed to comply with the Act in exercising a power` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the failure caused me a loss or brought them a profit` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I get compensation from my administrator`
`s 59(1)-(4) -- compensation or account of profits` `my administrator failed to comply with the Act in exercising a power` `the failure caused me a loss or brought them a profit` MEANS
    IF NOT `my administrator failed to comply with the Act in exercising a power`
    THEN `no: s 59 needs a failure by the administrator to comply with the Act in exercising a power`
    ELSE IF NOT `the failure caused me a loss or brought them a profit`
         THEN `no: the failure caused you no loss and brought the administrator no profit, so there is nothing for s 59 to order`
         ELSE `yes: the tribunal or a court may order the administrator to compensate your loss, or to account for their profits, but not both for the same exercise of power (s 59(1), (2)); this holds even if the appointment has ended or they were convicted (s 59(3), (4)); a court may relieve an administrator who acted honestly and reasonably (s 58); an amount paid counts against damages in a later civil claim (s 59(9))`

@export My administrator (or guardian) did not follow the Act with my money or affairs. Can I get compensation, or make them hand over their profits (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) ss 58, 59(1)-(4), (9))?
GIVEN `my administrator failed to comply with the Act in exercising a power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did your administrator or guardian fail to comply with the Act in exercising a power (for example an unauthorised conflict transaction, mixing your money with theirs, or not acting honestly and with reasonable diligence)?
      `the failure caused me a loss or brought them a profit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the failure cause you a loss, or bring the administrator a profit?
GIVETH A `Can I get compensation from my administrator`
DECIDE `Can I get compensation from my administrator question` IS
    `s 59(1)-(4) -- compensation or account of profits` `my administrator failed to comply with the Act in exercising a power` `the failure caused me a loss or brought them a profit`

-- s 59(5): "If the adult or appointee has died, an application for an order under subsection (1) must be made to the
-- tribunal or a court within 6 months after the death." (6): "If the adult and appointee have died, an application ...
-- must be made ... within 6 months after the first death." (7): "The tribunal or a court may extend the application
-- time." While both are alive, s 59 sets no time limit (NOTHING for the last day: the Act is silent, F8). A regulative
-- rule on the applicant, counted in days from the (first) death.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Guardianship party` `Guardianship act`
`s 59(5), (6) -- apply within 6 months after the death` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `apply for compensation or an account of profits`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "s 59(5), (6): the application was not made within 6 months after the death (or the first death); it is out of time unless the tribunal or court extends the time (s 59(7))"

GIVEN `I have died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I died` IS A DATE
      `my administrator has died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day my administrator died` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 59(5), (6) -- the day the 6 months start` `I have died` `the day I died` `my administrator has died` `the day my administrator died` MEANS
    IF `I have died` AND `my administrator has died`
    THEN JUST (`the earlier of` `the day I died` `and` `the day my administrator died`)
    ELSE IF `I have died`
         THEN JUST `the day I died`
         ELSE IF `my administrator has died`
              THEN JUST `the day my administrator died`
              ELSE NOTHING

GIVEN `I have died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I died` IS A DATE
      `my administrator has died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day my administrator died` IS A DATE
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`s 59(5), (6) -- the last day to apply` `I have died` `the day I died` `my administrator has died` `the day my administrator died` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 59(5), (6) -- the day the 6 months start` `I have died` `the day I died` `my administrator has died` `the day my administrator died`
    WHEN JUST `the start` THEN JUST (6 `months after` `the start`)
    WHEN NOTHING THEN NOTHING

@export By what day must the application for compensation from the administrator be made, if the adult or the administrator has died (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 59(5)-(7))? No answer means neither has died and s 59 sets no time limit. Asked by the adult's estate where the adult has died.
GIVEN `I have died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the adult whose affairs were administered died? (Answered by the estate's representative if so.)
      `the day I died` IS A DATE @desc The date the adult died (ISO 8601); if not, any date
      `my administrator has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the administrator (or guardian) died?
      `the day my administrator died` IS A DATE @desc The date the administrator died (ISO 8601); if not, any date
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
DECIDE `Last day to apply for compensation from my administrator question` IS
    `s 59(5), (6) -- the last day to apply` `I have died` `the day I died` `my administrator has died` `the day my administrator died`

DECLARE `Is the compensation application in time` IS ONE OF
    `s 59 sets no time limit while you and your administrator are both alive`
    `yes: made within 6 months after the death (s 59(5), (6))`
    `not yet due: the 6 months after the death have not ended (s 59(5), (6))`
    `in time only because the tribunal or court extended the time (s 59(7))`
    `out of time: the 6 months after the death have ended; ask the tribunal or court to extend the time (s 59(7))`

GIVEN `I have died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day I died` IS A DATE
      `my administrator has died` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day my administrator died` IS A DATE
      `the application has been made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE
      `the time has been extended` IS A BOOLEAN
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Is the compensation application in time`
`s 59(5)-(7) -- application in time` `I have died` `the day I died` `my administrator has died` `the day my administrator died` `the application has been made` `the day of the application` `the time has been extended` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER `s 59(5), (6) -- the day the 6 months start` `I have died` `the day I died` `my administrator has died` `the day my administrator died`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN `s 59 sets no time limit while you and your administrator are both alive`
    WHEN JUST `the start` THEN
        CONSIDER `the state of` `apply for compensation or an account of profits` `by` `the applicant` `under` (`s 59(5), (6) -- apply within 6 months after the death` (`the number of days from` `the start` `until` (6 `months after` `the start`))) `from` `the start` `done` `the application has been made` `on` `the day of the application` `as at` `today`
        WHEN `yes: it was done within the period the Act allows` THEN `yes: made within 6 months after the death (s 59(5), (6))`
        WHEN `not yet due: it has not been done, but the period has not ended` THEN `not yet due: the 6 months after the death have not ended (s 59(5), (6))`
        OTHERWISE
            IF `the time has been extended`
            THEN `in time only because the tribunal or court extended the time (s 59(7))`
            ELSE `out of time: the 6 months after the death have ended; ask the tribunal or court to extend the time (s 59(7))`

@export Is the application for compensation from the administrator in time (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 59(5)-(7))? Asked by the adult, or by the adult's estate where the adult has died.
GIVEN `I have died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the adult whose affairs were administered died? (Answered by the estate's representative if so.)
      `the day I died` IS A DATE @desc The date the adult died (ISO 8601); if not, any date
      `my administrator has died` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the administrator (or guardian) died?
      `the day my administrator died` IS A DATE @desc The date the administrator died (ISO 8601); if not, any date
      `the application has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the application to the tribunal or a court been made?
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE @desc The date the application was made (ISO 8601); if not yet, any date
      `the time has been extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the tribunal or a court extended the time for the application?
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `Is the compensation application in time`
DECIDE `Is my compensation application in time question` IS
    `s 59(5)-(7) -- application in time` `I have died` `the day I died` `my administrator has died` `the day my administrator died` `the application has been made` `the day of the application` `the time has been extended` `today`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 60 -- a benefit in the adult's estate lost by the administrator's dealing`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 60(1): "This section applies if a person's benefit in an adult's estate under the adult's will, on intestacy, or by
-- another disposition taking effect on the adult's death, is lost because of a sale or other dealing with the adult's
-- property by an administrator of the adult." (2): even if the person is that administrator. (3): "The person, or the
-- person's personal representative, may apply to the court for compensation out of the adult's estate." (4): "the
-- compensation must not be more than the value of the lost benefit". (5): Succession Act 1981 ss 41(2)-(8), (10), (11)
-- and 44 apply (that Act's time limits and procedure: not encoded here). Court: the Supreme Court. SEAT: the person who
-- would have taken the benefit.
DECLARE `Can I claim for a benefit lost from the estate` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the adult's estate, of no more than the value of the lost benefit; Succession Act 1981 ss 41 and 44 apply to the application (s 60(3)-(5))`
    `no: s 60 applies only to a benefit under the will, on intestacy, or by another disposition taking effect on the adult's death (s 60(1))`
    `no: s 60 applies only where the benefit was lost because of a sale or other dealing with the adult's property by the adult's administrator (s 60(1))`

GIVEN `I had a benefit under the will, on intestacy or by a disposition on death` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it was lost because the administrator sold or dealt with the property` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I claim for a benefit lost from the estate`
`s 60 -- lost benefit in the estate` `I had a benefit under the will, on intestacy or by a disposition on death` `it was lost because the administrator sold or dealt with the property` MEANS
    IF NOT `I had a benefit under the will, on intestacy or by a disposition on death`
    THEN `no: s 60 applies only to a benefit under the will, on intestacy, or by another disposition taking effect on the adult's death (s 60(1))`
    ELSE IF NOT `it was lost because the administrator sold or dealt with the property`
         THEN `no: s 60 applies only where the benefit was lost because of a sale or other dealing with the adult's property by the adult's administrator (s 60(1))`
         ELSE `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court for compensation out of the adult's estate, of no more than the value of the lost benefit; Succession Act 1981 ss 41 and 44 apply to the application (s 60(3)-(5))`

@export I was to inherit something from a person whose affairs were run by an administrator, but the administrator sold or dealt with it. Can I claim compensation from the estate (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 60)?
GIVEN `I had a benefit under the will, on intestacy or by a disposition on death` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did you have a benefit in the adult's estate under the adult's will, on intestacy, or by another disposition taking effect on the adult's death?
      `it was lost because the administrator sold or dealt with the property` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was the benefit lost because the adult's administrator sold or otherwise dealt with the adult's property? (It does not matter if you were the administrator.)
GIVETH A `Can I claim for a benefit lost from the estate`
DECIDE `Can I claim for a lost benefit in the estate question` IS
    `s 60 -- lost benefit in the estate` `I had a benefit under the will, on intestacy or by a disposition on death` `it was lost because the administrator sold or dealt with the property`

GIVEN `the value of the lost benefit` IS A NUMBER
      `the compensation sought` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 60(4) -- the most compensation` `the value of the lost benefit` `the compensation sought` MEANS
    IF `the compensation sought` AT MOST `the value of the lost benefit`
    THEN `the compensation sought`
    ELSE `the value of the lost benefit`

@export What is the most the court can order me for a benefit in the estate the administrator's dealing lost: the amount I seek, capped at the value of the lost benefit (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 60(4))?
GIVEN `the value of the lost benefit` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the benefit you lost, in dollars
      `the compensation sought` IS A NUMBER @desc The compensation you are seeking, in dollars
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `Most compensation for a lost benefit question` IS
    `s 60(4) -- the most compensation` `the value of the lost benefit` `the compensation sought`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 60A, 60C -- a beneficiary's interest in the proceeds, and the 6-month application`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 60A(1)-(3): a beneficiary under a deceased adult's will "has the same interest in any surplus money or other property
-- (the proceeds) arising from a sale ... or other dealing with, property under the powers given to an administrator as
-- the beneficiary would have had in the property ... if the ... dealing had not happened", and is also entitled to
-- traceable income from, and capital gain on, the proceeds; (5): subject to a s 60C(1) order. s 60C(1): an application
-- to the court to give effect to s 60A, or to avoid an "unjust and disproportionate advantage or ... disadvantage";
-- (2): by a beneficiary, a deceased beneficiary's personal representative, or the adult's personal representative;
-- (4): "An application under this section must be made to the court within 6 months after the adult's death." (5): "The
-- court may extend the application time." A regulative rule on the applicant, counted from the adult's death.
DECLARE `Who wants to apply under s 60C` IS ONE OF
    `a beneficiary under the adult's will`
    `the personal representative of a deceased beneficiary under the will`
    `the adult's personal representative`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Can I apply about the proceeds` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you have the same interest in the proceeds of the sale or dealing (with their traceable income and capital gain) as you had in the property, and may apply to the Supreme Court under s 60C within 6 months after the adult's death (ss 60A(2), (3), 60C(2), (4))`
    `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court under s 60C within 6 months after the adult's death (s 60C(2), (4))`
    `no: only a beneficiary under the will, a deceased beneficiary's personal representative, or the adult's personal representative may apply (s 60C(2))`

GIVEN `who applies` IS A `Who wants to apply under s 60C`
GIVETH A `Can I apply about the proceeds`
`ss 60A, 60C(2) -- the proceeds and who may apply` `who applies` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who applies`
    WHEN `a beneficiary under the adult's will` THEN `yes: you have the same interest in the proceeds of the sale or dealing (with their traceable income and capital gain) as you had in the property, and may apply to the Supreme Court under s 60C within 6 months after the adult's death (ss 60A(2), (3), 60C(2), (4))`
    WHEN `someone else` THEN `no: only a beneficiary under the will, a deceased beneficiary's personal representative, or the adult's personal representative may apply (s 60C(2))`
    OTHERWISE `yes: you may apply to the Supreme Court under s 60C within 6 months after the adult's death (s 60C(2), (4))`

@export The administrator of a person who has now died sold or dealt with property left to me in their will. What is my interest in the proceeds, and can I apply to the court about it (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) ss 60A, 60C)?
GIVEN `who applies` IS A `Who wants to apply under s 60C` @desc Who you are: a beneficiary under the adult's will; the personal representative of a deceased beneficiary; the adult's personal representative; or someone else
GIVETH A `Can I apply about the proceeds`
DECIDE `Can I apply about the proceeds question` IS
    `ss 60A, 60C(2) -- the proceeds and who may apply` `who applies`

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Guardianship party` `Guardianship act`
`s 60C(4) -- apply within 6 months after the adult's death` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the applicant`
    MUST `apply to confirm or vary the operation of s 60A`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the applicant` BECAUSE "s 60C(4): the application was not made within 6 months after the adult's death; it is out of time unless the court extends the time (s 60C(5))"

GIVEN `the day the adult died` IS A DATE
GIVETH A DATE
`s 60C(4) -- the last day to apply` `the day the adult died` MEANS 6 `months after` `the day the adult died`

@export By what day must an application to the court about the proceeds of property the administrator dealt with be made (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 60C(4))?
GIVEN `the day the adult died` IS A DATE @desc The date the adult whose property was administered died (ISO 8601)
GIVETH A DATE
DECIDE `Last day to apply about the proceeds question` IS
    `s 60C(4) -- the last day to apply` `the day the adult died`

DECLARE `Is the s 60C application in time` IS ONE OF
    `yes: made within 6 months after the adult's death (s 60C(4))`
    `not yet due: the 6 months after the adult's death have not ended (s 60C(4))`
    `in time only because the court extended the time (s 60C(5))`
    `out of time: the 6 months after the adult's death have ended; ask the court to extend the time (s 60C(5))`

GIVEN `the day the adult died` IS A DATE
      `the application has been made` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE
      `the time has been extended` IS A BOOLEAN
      `today` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `Is the s 60C application in time`
`s 60C(4), (5) -- application in time` `the day the adult died` `the application has been made` `the day of the application` `the time has been extended` `today` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the state of` `apply to confirm or vary the operation of s 60A` `by` `the applicant` `under` (`s 60C(4) -- apply within 6 months after the adult's death` (`the number of days from` `the day the adult died` `until` (`s 60C(4) -- the last day to apply` `the day the adult died`))) `from` `the day the adult died` `done` `the application has been made` `on` `the day of the application` `as at` `today`
    WHEN `yes: it was done within the period the Act allows` THEN `yes: made within 6 months after the adult's death (s 60C(4))`
    WHEN `not yet due: it has not been done, but the period has not ended` THEN `not yet due: the 6 months after the adult's death have not ended (s 60C(4))`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `the time has been extended`
        THEN `in time only because the court extended the time (s 60C(5))`
        ELSE `out of time: the 6 months after the adult's death have ended; ask the court to extend the time (s 60C(5))`

@export Is my application to the court about the proceeds of property the administrator dealt with in time (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 60C(4), (5))?
GIVEN `the day the adult died` IS A DATE @desc The date the adult whose property was administered died (ISO 8601)
      `the application has been made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the application been made to the Supreme Court?
      `the day of the application` IS A DATE @desc The date the application was made (ISO 8601); if not yet, any date
      `the time has been extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the court extended the time for the application?
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date (ISO 8601)
GIVETH AN `Is the s 60C application in time`
DECIDE `Is my application about the proceeds in time question` IS
    `s 60C(4), (5) -- application in time` `the day the adult died` `the application has been made` `the day of the application` `the time has been extended` `today`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 153 -- the administrator's accounts and an audit`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 153(1): "The tribunal may order an adult's administrator or adult's attorney for a financial matter to file in the
-- tribunal, and serve on the applicant, a summary of receipts and expenditure for the adult or more detailed accounts of
-- dealings and transactions for the adult." (2): it may order an audit, with a copy of the report to the applicant.
-- (3): "on its own initiative or on the application of the adult or another interested person." (4): "even if-- (a) the
-- administrator's appointment has ended; or (b) the enduring power of attorney has been revoked; or (c) the adult has
-- died." A closed list of applicants.
DECLARE `Who wants the accounts` IS ONE OF
    `the adult (me)`
    `an interested person for the adult`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `Can I get my administrator's accounts` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may ask the tribunal to order your administrator to file accounts and serve them on you, and to have them audited with the report given to you, even if the appointment has ended or the adult has died (s 153(1)-(4))`
    `no: only the adult or an interested person may apply; the tribunal may still act on its own initiative (s 153(3))`

GIVEN `who applies` IS A `Who wants the accounts`
GIVETH A `Can I get my administrator's accounts`
`s 153 -- records and audit` `who applies` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who applies`
    WHEN `someone else` THEN `no: only the adult or an interested person may apply; the tribunal may still act on its own initiative (s 153(3))`
    OTHERWISE `yes: you may ask the tribunal to order your administrator to file accounts and serve them on you, and to have them audited with the report given to you, even if the appointment has ended or the adult has died (s 153(1)-(4))`

@export Can I make my administrator show me accounts of what they have done with my money, and have them audited (Guardianship and Administration Act 2000 (Qld) s 153)?
GIVEN `who applies` IS A `Who wants the accounts` @desc Who would apply: you (the adult); an interested person (someone with a sufficient and genuine concern for your rights and interests); or someone else
GIVETH A `Can I get my administrator's accounts`
DECIDE `Can I get my administrator's accounts question` IS
    `s 153 -- records and audit` `who applies`
